      en: "Gwadar is a rapidly transforming port city located on the southwestern coast of Balochistan along the Arabian Sea. Once a small fishing village owned by the Sultanate of Oman until 1958, it is now the crown jewel of the China-Pakistan Economic Corridor (CPEC). The city features a distinctive hammerhead-shaped peninsula that provides a natural deep-water harbor. Massive infrastructure projects, including modern port facilities and highways, are reshaping the local landscape, aiming to turn Gwadar into a major regional maritime hub. Despite rapid modernization, traditional wooden fishing boats still dot the older harbor, highlighting the city's transitional phase. Plizio curriculum connection: Economics & Geography K8 — deep-sea ports and international trade corridors."
